# `name` is a field SOME record declares, so the older check — which asks only
# whether any type has it — passes this straight through, and the read used to
# fail at run time with `error[runtime]: `point` has no field `name``.
#
# This language refuses before anything runs, so that run-time refusal was a
# hole rather than a design. It is closed for the case the checker can see
# without inference: `p` is bound to a bare construction, so the type in hand
# is plain to read off the statement above.
#
# A parameter still answers nothing here — the type it will hold is a question
# for whole-program inference, and this check stays quiet rather than guessing.
type point
  x
  y

type person
  name

pub play =
  p = point 1 2
  print "{p.name}"
